<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lautaro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1534–<a href="/years/1557">1557</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lautaro was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> military leader in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arauco-war">Arauco War</a>. Captured as a youth, he served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-valdivia">Pedro de Valdivia</a> as a groom and learned Spanish tactics, then escaped and led the Mapuche to victory at Tucapel in December 1553, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a> was killed. He won further battles and marched north towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, but was killed at Mataquito in 1557.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lautaro.jpg" alt="Lautaro" width="274" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GringoInChile · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lautaro_Ca%C3%B1ete.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lautaro, in Mapudungun Leftraru, meaning &quot;swift hawk&quot;, was the son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> lonko, a chief who holds office in peacetime, named Curiñancu. Captured by the Spaniards at about the age of eleven, he became the personal servant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-valdivia">Pedro de Valdivia</a>, and the Spaniards called him Felipe Lautaro because they found his name hard to pronounce. His tasks included caring for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a>'s horses and accompanying them into battle and military exercises, so that he lost any fear of horses and became a good rider. A captain, Marcos Veas, taught him the use of several weapons and cavalry tactics.</p><p>According to several historians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdivia">Valdivia</a> ordered the hands and feet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> of Curiñancu's group cut off near what is now Concepción, among them Lautaro's parents, as a lesson for resisting colonisation. Lautaro is said to have hidden the hatred this produced.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1553 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> met to decide how to respond to the Spanish invasion and chose war. The toqui <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caupolican">Caupolicán</a> named Lautaro vice toqui because, having served as a page among the Spanish cavalry, he knew how to defeat mounted conquistadors. Lautaro brought horses into Mapuche use, designed new tactics and organised a large, cohesive army, a formation unfamiliar to the Mapuche. With 6,000 warriors he attacked Fort Tucapel, whose garrison retreated to Purén. Valdivia tried to retake the fort with a reduced force, which was surrounded and massacred; Valdivia was captured and killed in December 1553.</p><p>A long victory celebration, required by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> tradition, delayed pursuit. In February 1554 Lautaro gathered 8,000 men and met a punitive expedition under Francisco de Villagra at the hill of Marihueñu, where he used four divisions, one held in reserve and one to cut off the retreat; only a small group of Spaniards escaped. He burned the abandoned Concepción. In 1555 he besieged the rebuilt city with 4,000 warriors, and only 38 Spaniards escaped by sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lautaro then planned to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>. His troops dwindled to about 600, but in October 1556 he reached the Mataquito River and made a fortified camp at Peteroa, where he repulsed Diego Cano and later held off Pedro de Villagra. At dawn on 29 April 1557 the Spaniards, who had learned the camp's layout from a Picunche informant, attacked from the hills of Caune. Lautaro was killed early in the fighting, and his head was displayed in the plaza of Santiago.</p><p>Alonso de Ercilla, who served in the Spanish forces, made Lautaro a central figure of his epic poem La Araucana. Chileans acclaim him as their first general, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-neruda">Pablo Neruda</a> wrote a poem about him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Francisco de Miranda</a> took his name for the Lautaro Lodge, a secret independence society.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lautaro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>San Martín's <b>Lautaro</b> Lodge and the Patriotic Society overthrew it on 8 October 1812 for the Second Triumvirate, which convened the General Constituent and Sovereign Assembly of 1813.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1553 the Mapuche elected Caupolicán as toqui and <b>Lautaro</b>, who had served with the Spanish cavalry, as his deputy; Lautaro built the first Mapuche cavalry and defeated and killed Valdivia at Tucapel, before his army was crushed at Mataquito in 1557.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mapuche">Mapuche · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1553 <b>Lautaro</b> defeated and killed Governor Pedro de Valdivia at Tucapel, and after the Battle of Curalaba in 1598 the uprising that followed destroyed or emptied the Spanish cities south of the Biobío River; that land, except for Chiloé, stayed free of Spanish rule.</blockquote><a href="/essays/before-the-state">Before the State</a></li><li><blockquote>Under leaders such as <b>Lautaro</b> and Caupolicán they defeated and killed Valdivia at Tucapel in 1553, beginning the long Arauco War.</blockquote><a href="/state/araucania">Araucanía</a></li><li><blockquote>He extended Spanish settlement south, founding Concepción and Valdivia, but was killed by the Mapuche under <b>Lautaro</b> at Tucapel in 1553.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-chile">Captaincy General of Chile</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tirúa, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. Died in Hualañé, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-38.33,-73.5&amp;year=1548" aria-label="Tirúa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2722 3540 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lautaro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3172 3810h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3172 3810h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-38.33,-73.5&amp;year=1548">What was here in 1548? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
